{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787155437,"nonce":"QJ3D0M6hqWroKzBHRNxVUfO2vAQ=","message":"**Follow-up to comment #1, which framed this as a four-way choice of rollover axis. That framing was wrong, and so was my next attempt at it. Reading the code gives a different question — a sharper one — and it is not about an axis.**\n\n**First, the small correction that still stands.** This ticket says *\"`7570090`'s `generation` has no advance trigger\"*. Today's trigger exists and is `internal/grouping/repository/group.go:211-238`: `COALESCE((SELECT max(prev.generation) …), 0) + 1` inside the INSERT, under `groups_key_gen_uniq`. It is an `alert_groups` mechanism end to end.\n\n**Second, the correction that matters — the Case lifecycle does NOT end a conversation today, and I claimed it did.** `internal/grouping/domain/lifecycle.go:70-73`, on `DefaultGroupCloseDelay`:\n\n\u003e *\"It is pinned EQUAL to `refire_grace`: closing this generation is what makes the next fire post a brand-new Slack root, so a close delay shorter than the re-fire grace hands a re-fire that oto classified as 'the same problem coming back' a new card anyway. It was 5m against a 10m grace, and the mismatch defeated the grace. See ADR 0026.\"*\n\nSo there are two axes today and they are deliberately coupled: `refire_grace` decides *is this the same problem coming back*, and `group_close_delay_s` decides *has the conversation gone quiet enough to end*. The pinning is the mechanism that makes the first one mean anything, and it was already got wrong once — 5m against a 10m grace.\n\n⛔ **Which means a conversation is NOT one Case today. It can hold several.** A re-fire inside `refire_grace` opens a new Case — `alerts/domain/lifecycle.go:114-118` is explicit that T8 is retired, *\"a closed Case is now strictly terminal\"*, and T7 is *\"the ONLY road out\"*; `reply.go:153-155` says the same from the render side, *\"a re-fire is a NEW case at the next `seq`, never a reopen of this one (ADR 0040)\"*. That new Case joins the still-open generation, and the operator sees the same card. That is the whole point of the grace.\n\n⭐⭐ **So the one-Case-per-conversation ruling is in direct tension with `refire_grace`, and that is the real question this ticket is standing on.** Laid out as a chain, every link read from the code above:\n\n1. every re-fire opens a new Case (ADR 0040, T8 retired) — SEEN\n2. one Case per conversation (the owner ruling on `7570090`)\n3. therefore every re-fire is a new conversation\n4. therefore every re-fire gets a brand-new Slack root\n5. therefore `refire_grace` can never suppress a new card again — and suppressing exactly that is what `lifecycle.go:70-73` says it is for\n\n**The invariant becomes unsatisfiable by construction, not by tuning.** No choice of rollover axis fixes that, which is why comment #1's four-option table was answering the wrong question.\n\n**The question that actually needs an answer, and only the owner can give it:**\n\n\u003e Does `refire_grace` survive the one-Case-per-conversation ruling?\n\nThree coherent answers, and they lead to completely different work:\n\n- **`refire_grace` dies with `alert_groups`.** Every re-fire gets its own card, and the \"same problem coming back\" concept is withdrawn. Simple and honest, and it means accepting that a flapping alert produces a card per flap — which is the noise the grace was introduced to remove. `refire_grace_s` then leaves the settings surface, the same way the reminder's four keys did.\n- **`refire_grace` survives, and a conversation may hold several Cases after all** — one *primary* Case plus its re-fires. Then \"one Case per conversation\" is really \"one primary Case\", the rollover rule is `group_close_delay_s` pinned to the grace exactly as it is today, and this ticket collapses to a rename.\n- **`refire_grace` moves up a layer** and gates whether a new Case *starts a new conversation* rather than whether it reopens the old one. Closest to the current behaviour with the ruling intact, and the only one that needs new mechanism.\n\n⚠️ **What I could not settle, and it bounds all of the above.** What happens to a re-fire *inside* `refire_grace` now that T8 is retired — a new Case joining the open generation, or nothing at all? T7's own doc comment reads *\"a re-fire beyond refire_grace\"*, which leaves the inside-grace path unstated in the place I would expect it stated. Worth one look at `alerts/service/lifecycle.go` before answering, because if the answer is \"nothing at all\" then the grace already only defers a card rather than merging anything, and option 1 costs much less than it appears to.\n\n**Separately, and unchanged: the thread-length problem is already solved and is not rollover.** `notification/domain/thread.go:136` `MaxThreadReplies = 30`, `:183` `NeedsFreshRoot()`, consumed at `notification/service/dispatch.go:770`, rendering as *\"Continued in a new message — this thread reached 30 replies\"*. It re-roots while preserving conversation identity, and it should stay nested inside whatever answers the above rather than being promoted into the rollover axis. Comment #1 offered promoting it as option (b) — that was a mistake for the same reason: it answers legibility, not identity.\n\n⛔ **Do not pick an axis on the strength of comment #1.** It is superseded by this. Nothing here is decided or built.","files":null}]}